// Book Six, Chapter 6 (session 64). Thursday in May 1924: the Parley of the houses at the Calloway Room.
// Opens with the day of preparation (one night phase of two actions out of three: the family's counsel, Marcel and the figure, the sheet of music), then a fixed Parley: the arrival, the five seats, the claim, the test of strength, the man in gloves, then a short dawn.
// The coven fronts it: the family dresses and stands behind the chair, and the sired and the hero show their strength. Names are always checked with in_circle().
// Reads b6.invite, b6.circle, b6.crates, b6.yards, b6.pell, b6.ames, b6.rivals, b6.song, b6.rudek, b6.tallis, b6.benefit. Sets b6.claim, b6.test and b6.cantor.
// Uses meet(), discover(), chart(), has_gift(), gift_holder(), power(). Ends with rest() and => next.
@chapter b6c06
@title The Calloway Room
@book relics-lost-god
@version 1

# start
## The Thursday
$ b6.claim = "peace"
$ b6.test = "answered"
$ b6.cantor = "asked"
$ _figure = 0
$ _lead = ""
$ _reader_name = "Dora Valer"
?if coven_size() > 0
  $ _lead = business_lead()
?end
?if _lead != ""
  $ _reader_name = name(_lead)
?end
~> thursday
---
$ do phase("night", 2)
It is Thursday, and the sun is going down behind the stockyards like a coin into a slot. There are three things that could be done before eleven, and time for two. {cap(voice("hunger"))}.
-> prep

# thursday
?if b6.invite == "declined"
  The Loop did not take the refusal as an insult. It took it as information. By Wednesday noon the whole city had heard that Mr. Collins would not come, and by Wednesday night a second card had arrived, much shorter, saying that the Parley would sit on Thursday regardless, and that his chair would remain empty, in case.
?elif b6.invite == "family"
  The word has gone round that the Exchange will come with its family. It has gone round very quickly. It has produced, from every house in the city, a stiff little silence, and one telephone call, from Mr. Ames, to ask how many.
?else
  The word has gone round that Mr. Collins will come. Mr. Ames has called, to ask whether he can be of use. Mrs. Pell has sent a note, to say that she will be present, and that she hopes it will not be tiresome.
?end
?if _lead != ""
  {_reader_name} has had the carriage brought round, and the coats brushed, and a long table of sandwiches laid for people who will not eat them, because it is a thing that one does. {pg(_lead, "He", "She", "They")} {pg(_lead, "says", "says", "say")} that a house is never so well governed as on the eve of a great trouble, and that it is an excellent night for it.
?else
  Dora has had the cab brought round, and your coat brushed, and has said, quite flatly, that she will wait in the street all night if she has to.
?end
<~

# prep
:prep
?if actions_left() == 2
  There is an hour or two before the sun is quite gone, and the whole house has the hushed look of a ship before a gale.
?else
  The streetlamps have come on. It is nearly time.
?end
* Sit with the family, and ask what each would say [once] ?if actions_left() > 0
  ~> family
  $ do act()
* Go down to the club, and have Marcel play the figure [once] ?if actions_left() > 0
  ~> marcel
  $ do act()
* Study the sheet of music, alone, with a lamp [once] ?if actions_left() > 0
  ~> sheet
  $ do act()
?if actions_left() <= 0
  $ do end_phase()
  -> go
?end
-> :prep

# family
## The counsel
?if coven_size() > 0
  They are in the long front room, with the shutters closed and a lamp lit, because it is the thing that one does, and because none of them has ever been in a room like it. {_reader_name} has put a chair for you. Nobody sits in it.
  ?if in_circle("fiammetta") and member("fiammetta", "status") == "active"
    Fiammetta has been to the Loop already, in a man's cap, and has drawn the street door and the two back ones, and the fire escape on a menu card. She says that the room has no windows. She says that this is either very safe or very foolish.
  ?end
  ?if in_circle("thibault") and member("thibault", "status") == "active"
    Thibault has found the telephone man, who is paid by three houses, and has found that he will take a fourth. He says that tonight, for the first time in the history of Chicago, there will be a line from the Calloway Room to this front room, and that Miss Calloway will never know.
  ?end
  ?if in_circle("hanno") and member("hanno", "status") == "active"
    Hanno has made a list, in his small square hand, of what each house owes, and to whom, and has found that there is not one of the four that is not in debt to another. It is, he says, a very well balanced city.
  ?end
  ?if gift_holder("memory") != ""
    {name(gift_holder("memory"))} recites the names of everyone who has ever sat at the Calloway Room's long table, with the dates, and who sat on whose right, and who has been moved. It takes twenty minutes. At the end you know, without having been told, where to put your feet.
  ?end
  They do not tell you what to do. It is the thing that you are, rather slowly, coming to love in them. They tell you what they have seen, and what they are afraid of, and what they would not have you leave out. It is a family's counsel, and it is a very quiet thing, and it is, you notice, a great deal heavier than an order.
  $ standing += 1
  $ humanity += 1
?else
  Dora sits across the long table with a pencil, and goes through the room in order, as she has had it from her grandmother's cousin, who was once a waitress at the Calloway. She says that the chairs are in a half circle. She says that the whole of the thing will turn on who sits, and who does not, and who is made to wait.
  $ standing += 1
?end
<~

# marcel
## The figure
$ do chart("chicago.club")
$ do meet("marcel")
Prosper's is closed to the public on a Thursday, and the chairs are on the tables. Marcel is sitting alone on the bandstand in his shirt sleeves, with the clarinet across his knee, and he has been waiting an hour.
He plays it for you. It is, after all, the most that he can do. The six notes rise, and rise, and the seventh does not come where it should. It lies, a half step flat, like a shadow thrown by something that is not in the room. He plays it again. Then he lowers the clarinet, and says that the song has a place for a voice. He says that his grandfather's grandfather said it was a part that could not be played, and could not be taught. It had to be *given*, he said, by someone who was owed it.
$ _figure = 1
?if b6.song == "spoke"
  He says that the man in gloves is the only person who ever listened to the seventh, instead of covering it.
?end
$ standing += 1
<~

# sheet
## The seventh note
You sit with the sheet in the lamplight, at the kitchen table, for an hour. It is in a small, neat, accurate hand. It has six notes, a seventh, and a mark beside the seventh that is not a clef or a rest or a fermata. It is, you slowly realise, the red ray. It is a very small drawing of it.
$ do discover("the_six_rays")
?if has_gift("old_blood")
  The old blood in you hears it before you read it. It is a held breath, rather than a song. It is the shape of a gap that something is going to fit into.
?else
  You do not know what it is. But you have the oddest feeling that you have been humming it, without knowing, for some weeks.
?end
$ _figure = 1
<~

# go
## The Calloway Room
$ do chart("chicago.goldcoast")
~> arrive
---
~> seats
---
~> claim
---
~> test
---
